  "body": "## What changed\n- **Post rule.** `arcFateOf` (`crates/post/src/post/arc_guard.rs`) has one more rule. The written start and the written end differ, but they are on one ray from the written centre (the same angle). Or one end is on the written centre. If the record turns half a circle or less, the answer is `\"linear\"`. The post writes its straight feed move.\n- The written centre is the centre that the control computes: the written start plus the written `I J K` increment (the script passes `inc`). The Heidenhain posts write an absolute centre and pass no `inc`.\n- The rule reads the numbers of the written words. It uses only the zero of the cross product and the sign of the dot product. A mirrored axis or a lathe diameter word does not change these.\n- A record whose own ends in the stream are on one ray (stream sweep 0) stays an arc in the post. `emitArcWithRadius` reads that as a full turn.\n- **Stream writer rule (second review).** `cnc_arc_lattice::short_arc_on_lattice` (called by `cnc::serialize`) has the same rule on the micrometre lattice. The rounded ends differ, they are on one ray from the rounded centre (or one is on it), and the true sweep is positive and not more than half a turn. The writer then gives a `Feed` to the rounded end. A turn of more than half a circle keeps its arc record.\n- `fanuc-lathe.cps` and `tormach-lathe.cps` now write the feed move for `\"linear\"` in their meridian arc path. Before, they wrote no block for any answer that was not `\"arc\"`.\n- The full-turn test keeps main's exact stream endpoint equality.\n- Decision: `docs/decisions/3474-same-angle-arc.md`.\n\n## Cause\nThe #3442 guard compares only the written ends. When the ends differ only along the radius, the guard returned `\"arc\"`. Example, Fanuc mm: `G01 X10.011 Y5.` then `G03 X10.01 Y5. Z5.5 I-0.011 J0. F180`.\n\nThe start and the end are at the same angle about the centre. The radii are 0.011 and 0.010. RS-274 / ISO 6983 reads an arc whose end is at its start angle as a full turn. No control ran these blocks. A straight move is correct for each reading.\n\nThe app posts through `cnc::serialize`, which writes whole micrometres. A mm post with three decimals writes those numbers again. For the arc of the issue, the stream has a zero sweep, and the post cannot tell a short arc from a turn of 359.99 degrees. Only the writer knows the true sweep. Thus the writer has the rule too.\n\n## Tests\n`crates/post/tests/same_angle_stream.rs` (new, each test starts from unrounded geometry and goes through `serialize`):\n- `the_arc_of_3474_through_the_stream_writer_posts_as_a_straight_move_on_fanuc`: a `Toolpath` through `dump` and `cnc::serialize`, Fanuc in mm and inch. No arc block, `G01` to the written end, byte-equal to the straight feed.\n- `every_mill_post_writes_the_arc_of_3474_through_the_stream_writer_as_its_straight_move`: 15 mill posts in each unit that they offer.\n- `the_lathes_post_a_same_angle_meridian_arc_through_the_stream_writer_as_a_straight_move`: `fanuc-lathe` and `tormach-lathe`, mm.\n- `the_long_way_round_keeps_its_arc_through_the_stream_writer`: the same ends CW stay one arc block.\n\n`crates/post/tests/cnc_arc_lattice.rs`:\n- `a_short_arc_whose_rounded_ends_share_one_angle_is_written_as_a_feed`: XY, a helix, an end on the centre, XZ and YZ.\n- `a_long_turn_an_opposite_end_and_a_visible_arc_keep_their_arc_record_on_one_line`.\n\n`crates/post/tests/same_angle_arcs.rs` (the post rule on hand-written streams):\n- `fanuc_and_linuxcnc_post_a_same_angle_arc_as_a_straight_move`\n- `every_post_writes_a_same_angle_arc_as_its_straight_move`\n- `a_same_angle_arc_about_the_written_incremental_centre_posts_as_its_straight_move`\n- `arc_fate_reads_a_same_angle_short_arc_as_a_straight_move`\n\n### Pin proof (on head `927a883be`)\n- The writer ignores the ray rule: red. `same_angle_stream` fails 3 tests (Fanuc, each mill post, the lathes). `cnc_arc_lattice` fails 1 test. Restore: 4 + 4 passed.\n- `_arcWrittenCentre` ignores `inc`: red (`same_angle_arcs`, 2 tests). Checked on `8f81cb1c`.\n- The `1e-5` full-turn band is put back: red (`coincident_arcs`, 2 tests). Checked on `8f81cb1c`.\n\n### Gates (on head `927a883be`, base `upstream/main` `4dba7019d`)\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main`: 252 steps, 0 failed.\n- `cargo xtask gate heavy --base upstream/main`: 213 steps, 0 failed, 35.0 min.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-post --all-targets -- -D warnings`: passed.\n- `cargo test -p camcraft-app --test example_catalog`: 37 passed, 4 ignored. `example_catalog_adaptive`: 1 passed. `example_catalog_edge_work`: 1 passed. `short_arc_lattice`: 2 passed.\n- `same_angle_arcs` 4 passed; `coincident_arcs` 8 passed; `radius_arcs` 7 passed.\n\nThe gates ran on the PR head, not on a merge with main. `git merge-tree` with `upstream/main` is clean.\n\n## Goldens moved\nNone. The catalog suites, the quick gate and the heavy lanes ran on the final head. No recorded program (`.nc`, `.cl`, `examples/catalog/**`) changed.\n\n## Not exercised\n- No control ran the blocks. The reading of the controls is from RS-274 / ISO 6983, not from a run.\n- A written end behind the written start (a written sweep of nearly a full turn on a short record). We found no input that makes it. It is recorded as dropped in the decision.\n- Plasma and wire posts get the two rules through the same `arcFate` call and the same writer. They have no witness of their own. There is no inch lathe witness.\n- A straight move of one or two output steps under active cutter compensation (G41/G42) was not checked on a control.\n- No UI change: no screenshots.\n\nChangelog: A post now writes a straight move for a very small arc whose written start and end have the same angle about the centre. Before, a control could read the block as a full turn.\n\nFixes #3474\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
